<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-9-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Properties of Lines</title>

<assemblage component="instructor">
<p>In this Lesson we review the properties of horizontal and vertical lines, and parallel and perpendicular lines.</p>

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Find equations for horizontal and vertical lines </p></li>   
    <li><p>Decide whether two lines are parallel, perpendicular, or neither</p></li>
    <li><p>Find equations for parallel or perpendicular lines </p></li>
</ul></p></assemblage> 

<activity><title>Horizontal and Vertical Lines</title>

          <aside component="instructor"><p>1(a) Some students will confuse the equation of a horizontal or vertical line with its slope.</p></aside>
<p><ol marker="a.">
  <li><p>Graph <m>3y=15</m>.</p> 
        <image source="act-9-1-1.png" width="35%"><description>Grid [-6,6]x[-6,6]</description></image>
    <p>Compute its slope.  <m>~~m=</m></p>
    <p>Illustrate <m>\Delta x</m> and <m>\Delta y</m> on your graph.</p></li>   

  <li><p>Graph <m>2x=-4</m>.</p>
        <image source="act-9-1-1.png" width="35%"><description>Grid [-6,6]x[-6,6]</description></image>
    <p>Compute its slope. <m>~~m=</m></p>     
    <p>Illustrate <m>\Delta x</m> and <m>\Delta y</m> on your graph.</p></li>

  <li><p>The slope of a horizontal line is  <fillin/>.</p>                                     .
      <p>The slope of a vertical line is  <fillin/>.</p></li>

  <li><p>Make a sketch of each line, then find its equation. </p>
  
              <aside component="instructor"><p>1(d) Students may have difficulty with this task. Sketching the line described will help them visualize it, and hence find its equation.</p></aside>
    <p><ol marker="1.">
      <li><p>A horizontal line through  <m>(2,-4)</m></p>
          <image source="act-9-1-1.png" width="35%"><description>Grid [-6,6]x[-6,6]</description></image></li>
      <li><p>The <m>y</m>-axis</p>
          <image source="act-9-1-1.png" width="35%"><description>Grid [-6,6]x[-6,6]</description></image></li>
      <li><p>Parallel to the <m>y</m>-axis and including <m>(-1,-2)</m></p>
          <image source="act-9-1-1.png" width="35%"><description>Grid [-6,6]x[-6,6]</description></image></li>
      <li><p>Perpendicular to <m>x=3</m>, intersecting it at <m>(3,5)</m></p>
          <image source="act-9-1-1.png" width="35%"><description>Grid [-6,6]x[-6,6]</description></image></li>
      </ol></p></li>
  </ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Parallel and Perpendicular Lines</title>

<p><ol marker="1.">
<li><p>The slopes of several lines are given below. Which of the lines are parallel to the graph of <m>y=2.5x-3</m>, and which are perpendicular to it? Which are neither? Support each answer with a calculation.</p>

              <aside component="instructor"><p>2(1) This task is just an exercise in fractions, but worth going over.</p></aside>

<p><ol cols="2" marker="a.">
  <li><p><m>m=\dfrac{2}{5}</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>m=\dfrac{25}{10}</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>m=\dfrac{-8}{20}</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>m=\dfrac{-45}{18}</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>m=\dfrac{40}{16}</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>m=25</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>m=\dfrac{-1}{25}</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>m=\dfrac{-5}{10}</m></p></li>
</ol></p></li>

<li><p>In each part, determine whether the two lines are parallel, perpendicular, or neither.</p>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>2(2) Students may confuse the equation of a line and its slope.</p></aside>

<p><ol marker="a.">
  <li><p><m>2x-7y = 14;~~~7x-2y = 14</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>x+y = 6;~~~ x-y = 6</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>x=-3;~~~ 3y=5</m></p></li>
  <li><p><m>2y=5;~~~ 5y=-2</m></p></li>
</ol></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity><title>Applications</title>  

<p><ol marker="1.">
<li><p>Altitude of a Triangle</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
      <li><p>Sketch the triangle with vertices <m>A(-6,-3),~B(-6,3),~</m> and <m>~C(4,5)</m>.</p>
            <image source="act-2-2-2.png" width="50%"><description>10x10 Grid</description></image></li>

      <li><p>Find the slope of the side <m>\overline{AC}</m>.</p>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>3(1c) You may need to remind students what the altitude of a triangle is.</p></aside></li>

      <li><p>Sketch the altitude from point <m>B</m> to side <m>\overline{AC}</m>. Find the slope of the altitude.</p></li>

      <li><p>Find an equation for the line that includes the altitude from point <m>B</m> to side <m>\overline{AC}</m>. (Hint:  You know a point <m>(x,y)</m> that lies on the line.)</p></li>
    </ol></p></li>

<li><p>Finding a Parallel Line</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
      <li><p>Write the equation <m>2y-3x=5</m> in slope-intercept form, and graph the equation.</p>
          <image source="act-2-2-2.png" width="50%"><description>10x10 Grid</description></image></li>

      <li><p>What is the slope of any line that is parallel to <m>2y-3x=5~</m>?</p></li>

      <li><p>On your graph for part (a), sketch by hand a line that is parallel to <m>2y-3x=5~</m> and passes through the point <m>(-3,2)</m>.</p></li>

      <li><p>Use the point-slope formula to write an equation for the line that is parallel to the graph of <m>2y-3x=5~</m> and passes through the point <m>(-3,2)</m>.</p></li>
    </ol></p></li>

  <li><p>Finding a Perpendicular Line</p>
      <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><p>Write the equation <m>x-2y=5</m> in slope-intercept form, and graph the equation.</p>
              <image source="act-2-2-2.png" width="50%"><description>10x10 Grid</description></image></li>

      <li><p>What is the slope of any line that is parallel to <m>x-2y=5~</m>?</p></li>

      <li><p>On your graph for part (a), sketch by hand a line that is parallel to <m>x-2y=5~</m> and passes through the point <m>(4,-3)</m>.</p></li>

      <li><p>Use the point-slope formula to write an equation for the line that is parallel to the graph of <m>x-2y=5~</m> and passes through the point <m>(4,-3)</m>.</p></li>
    </ol></p></li>
  </ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>How can you calculate the reciprocal of a decimal number?</p></li>
    <li><p>Compare the graphs of the lines <m>y=\frac{2}{5}</m> and <m>y=\frac{2}{5}x</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>What two things do you need to know about a line in order to write its equation?</p></li>
    <li><p>If you know the slope of the base of a triangle, how do you find the slope of its altitude?</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Explain why the equation of a vertical line is <m>x=k</m>, where <m>k</m> is a constant.</p></li>
    <li><p>Compare the graphs of the lines <m>y=-\frac{3}{4}</m>  and <m>y=-\frac{3}{4}x</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Explain how to test whether the graphs of two linear equations are perpendicular.</p></li>
    <li><p>Are all lines the graphs of functions? Explain.</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>When is the graph of <m>Ax+By=C</m> a vertical line?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>If <m>A=0</m></li>
        <li>If <m>B=0</m></li>
        <li>If <m>C=0</m></li>
        <li>Never</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The equation of the <m>x</m>-axis is</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>x=0</m></li>
        <li><m>y=0</m></li>
        <li><m>m=0</m></li>
        <li>undefined</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The base of a triangle has slope <m>\dfrac{2}{3}</m>.  Its altitude has slope</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>\dfrac{2}{3}</m></li>
        <li><m>\dfrac{-2}{3}</m></li>
        <li><m>\dfrac{3}{2}</m></li>
        <li><m>\dfrac{-3}{2}</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>If <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are negative reciprocals, then their product is</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>0</m></li>
        <li><m>1</m></li>
        <li><m>-1</m></li>
        <li>undefined</li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>